Albert Camus

Albert Camus was an extremely handsome mid-twentieth-century French-Algerian
philosopher and writer whose claim to our attention rests on three novels (The
Outsider, The Plague and The Fall) and two philosophical essays (The Myth of
Sisyphus and The Rebel). Camus won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957 and
died at the age of 46. He was inadvertently killed by his publisher, Michel
Gallimard, when the Facel Vega sports car in which they were travelling crashed
into a tree. In his pocket was a train ticket he had decided not to use at the
last minute.

Camus's fame began with, and still largely rests on, his 1942 novel, The
Outsider. Set in Camus's native Algiers, it tells the story of a laconic,
detached and ironic hero called Meursault. He is a man who cannot see the point
of love, work or friendship, and who one day, somewhat by mistake, shoots an
Arab man dead without understanding his own motives. He ends up being put to
death partly because he shows no remorse, although he seems largely indifferent
to his fate either way. The novel captures the state of mind that the
sociologist Émile Durkheim defined as 'anomie': a listless, affectless,
alienated condition in which one feels entirely cut off from others and cannot
find a way to share their sympathies or values. Reading The Outsider has long
been a well-known adolescent rite of passage among French and many other
teenagers. This is not to diminish the book, since many of its greatest themes
are first tackled at the age of 17 or so.

The hero of The Outsider, Meursault, cannot accept any of the standard answers
to why things are as they are. He sees hypocrisy and sentimentality everywhere
and cannot overlook them. He is a man who cannot accept the conventional
explanations offered for things such as the education system, the workplace,
relationships or the workings of government. He stands outside ordinary
bourgeois life, highly critical of its pinched morality and narrow concerns with
money and family. As Camus put it in an afterword to the American edition of the
book: 'Meursault doesn't play the game. He refuses to lie ... he says what he
is; he refuses to hide his feelings ... and so society immediately feels
threatened.' Much of the book's unusual, mesmerising quality comes from the
coolly distant voice in which Meursault speaks to us, his readers. Its opening
is one of the most memorable in twentieth-century literature, and sets the tone.

'Aujourd’hui, maman est morte. Ou peut-être hier, je ne sais pas.' The ending is
stark and defiant. Meursault, who is condemned to death for a murder committed
almost offhandedly because he is curious to know what it is like to pull the
trigger, rejects all consolations and heroically accepts the universe's total
indifference to humankind. 'My last wish was that there should be a crowd of
spectators at my execution ... and that they should greet me with cries of
hatred.' Even if we are not killers, and would ourselves be deeply saddened when
our mothers die, the mood of The Outsider is one we are all likely to recognise.
It is the feeling of having enough freedom to realise that we are in a cage, but
not quite enough freedom to escape it; of feeling that no one understands us and
that everything appears a little hopeless, perhaps in the summer before we go to
college.

Aside from The Outsider, Camus's fame rests on an essay published in the same
year as the novel, entitled The Myth of Sisyphus. This essay, too, has a bold
beginning: 'There is but one truly serious philosophical problem ... and that is
suicide. Judging whether life is or is not worth living ... that is the
fundamental question of philosophy.' The reason for this stark choice, in
Camus's eyes, is that as soon as we start to think seriously, as philosophers
do, we will see that life has no meaning. We will therefore be compelled to
wonder whether or not we should simply end it all.

To make sense of this rather extreme claim, we have to situate Camus in the
history of thought. His dramatic announcement that we have to consider killing
ourselves because life might be meaningless is premised on an earlier notion:
that life could actually be rich in God-given meaning. This concept may sound
remote to many of us today. Yet we have to bear in mind that, for the last two
thousand years in the West, the idea that life was meaningful was taken for
granted, its authority deriving above all from one institution: the Christian
Church. Camus stands in a long line of thinkers, from Kierkegaard and Nietzsche
to Heidegger and Sartre, who wrestle with the chilling realisation that there
is, in fact, no preordained meaning in life. We are just biological matter
spinning senselessly on a tiny rock in a corner of an indifferent universe. We
were not put here by a benevolent deity and asked to work towards salvation by
following the Ten Commandments. There is no roadmap and no greater purpose. It
is this realisation that lies at the heart of so many of the crises described by
the thinkers we now know as the existentialists.

A child of despairing modernity, Albert Camus accepts that our lives are all
absurd in the grand scheme of things but, unlike some philosophers, ultimately
resists utter hopelessness or nihilism. He argues that we have to live with the
knowledge that our efforts will be largely futile, our lives soon forgotten, and
our species irredeemably corrupt and violent, and yet we should endure
nevertheless. We are like Sisyphus, the Greek figure condemned by the gods to
roll a boulder up a mountain, only to watch it fall back down again in
perpetuity.

Ultimately, Camus suggests that we should cope as best we can with whatever we
have to do. We have to acknowledge the absurdity underlying existence and then
triumph over the constant possibility of hopelessness. In his famous
formulation, 'One must imagine Sisyphus happy.' This brings us to the most
charming and seductive side of Camus: he wants to remind himself and us of the
reasons why life can be worth enduring. In the process, he writes with
exceptional intensity and wisdom about relationships, nature, summer, food and
friendship. As a guide to the reasons for living, Camus is delightful. Many
philosophers have been ugly and cut off from their bodies: think of the sickly
Pascal, the crippled Leopardi, the sexually unsuccessful Schopenhauer or the
poor, peculiar Nietzsche. Camus, by contrast, was very good-looking and
extremely successful with women. During the last ten years of his life, he never
had fewer than three girlfriends on the go, and he was married as well. He also
had a great dress sense, influenced by James Dean and Humphrey Bogart. It is not
surprising that he was asked to pose for American Vogue.

These were not merely stylistic quirks. Once you properly realise that life is
absurd, you may be on the verge of despair, but you are also compelled to live
more intensely. Accordingly, Camus became deeply committed to the pleasures of
ordinary life. He said that he saw his philosophy as 'A lucid invitation to live
and to create in the very midst of the desert.' He was a great champion of the
ordinary, which generally has a hard time finding champions in philosophy. After
pages and pages of dense philosophy, one turns with relief to moments when Camus
writes simply in praise of sunshine, kissing or dancing. He was an outstanding
athlete as a young man. When his friend Charles Poncet once asked him which he
preferred, football or the theatre, Camus is said to have replied: 'Football,
without hesitation.'

Camus played as a goalkeeper for the local junior team in Algiers, Racing
Universitaire d'Alger, which won both the North African Champions Cup and the
North African Cup in the 1930s. The sense of team spirit, fraternity and common
purpose appealed enormously to Camus. When a sports magazine asked him in the
1950s to say a few words about his time playing football, he said: 'After many
years during which I saw many things ... what I know most surely about morality
and the duty of man ... I owe to sport.' Camus was also a great advocate of the
sun. His beautiful essay, 'Summer in Algiers', celebrates the warmth of the
water and the brown bodies of women. He writes: 'For the first time in two
thousand years, the body has appeared naked on beaches. For twenty centuries,
men have striven to give decency to Greek insolence, a naivety to diminish the
flesh and complicate dress. But today, young men running on Mediterranean
beaches repeat the gestures of the athletes of Delos.'

He spoke up for a new paganism based on the immediate pleasures of the body.
Here is an extract from 'Summer in Algiers': 'I recall a magnificent, tall girl
who danced all afternoon. She was wearing a jasmine garland and a tight blue
dress, which was wet with perspiration from the small of her back down to her
legs. She was laughing as she danced and throwing back her head as she passed
the tables. She left behind her a mingled scent of flowers and flesh.' Camus
railed against those who dismissed such things as trivial and longed for
something higher, better and purer. 'If there is a sin against this life ...' he
wrote, 'it consists perhaps not so much in sparing life as in hoping for another
life and eluding the quiet grandeur of this one.'

In a letter, he remarked: 'People attract me insofar as they are impassioned
about life and avid for happiness ... There are causes worth dying for, but none
worth killing for.' Camus achieved huge acclaim in his lifetime, but the
Parisian intellectual community was deeply suspicious of him. He was never a
Parisian sophisticate; he was a working-class pied-noir, that is, someone born
in Algeria but of European origin, whose father had died of war wounds when he
was an infant and whose mother was a cleaning lady. It is no coincidence that
Camus's favourite philosopher was Montaigne, another very down-to-earth
Frenchman, and someone one can love as much for what he wrote as for what he was
like. He was someone one would have wanted as a wise, life-enhancing friend.
This, too, is what philosophy is about.